import '../styles/chart.css';
---

<Essay
  docNo="02"
  title="How a product earns confidence"
  description="How much evidence is enough: sample size, the tail of the distribution, and the explanation behind a failure."
>
  <div class="sub">
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p class="opener">How much evidence is enough?</p>
      <p>
        Every NPI build ends with someone deciding to move on. My work is the evidence behind that
        decision, and the hard part is knowing when there is enough of it.
      </p>
      <p>Statistics answers most of that question. Not all of it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">2.1</span>
      <h2>Three passes are not proof</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>Three units pass a test. It feels like good news. Statistically, it says very little.</p>
      <p>
        To claim 90% reliability at 90% confidence, 22 units have to chartive the test with zero
        failures. Three passes support a claim of about 46% at the same confidence.
      </p>
      <p>
        The count only matters if the measurement can be trusted, which is why Gage R&amp;R comes
        first. Sample size is not a formality. It sets what a result is allowed to mean.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        From the success-run theorem, n = ln(1 − C) / ln(R).
        <a href="https://accendoreliability.com/sample-size-success-testing/" target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Reference ↗</a
        >
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">2.2</span>
      <h2>The mean can pass while the tail fails</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        Failures do not arrive evenly. Over a product's life, the failure rate traces a bathtub:
        early failures from defects that escaped the line, a flat stretch of random failures, then
        wear-out.
      </p>
