      <p>
        Each region needs different evidence. Screening and build quality catch early failures. Life
        tests prove wear-out. A model that assumes the flat bottom, like the exponential, can miss
        both ends.
      </p>
      <p>
        The mean is where statistics is most reassuring and least useful. In one life test we ran in
        the lab, 70 units cycled to failure. Three models fit the data with the same MTTF, about
        21,500 cycles. They disagreed on when the first 10% would fail by more than four times.
        The Weibull shape, β ≈ 2.3, puts this data in wear-out, which is why the exponential fits
        worst.
      </p>
